WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.080
Controlling an
autonomous vehicle

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.020
in order to accomplish a task
requires an understanding

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.860
of many aspects of the problem,
from the vehicle's dynamics,

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:16.239
to obstacles to avoid, to
the values of each objective.

00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:18.520
It can be inefficient,
error-prone,

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.560
or impossible for
human operators

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.740
to specify a detailed
action sequence.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:27.100
Thus, it is important to
elevate the level of commanding,

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.730
such that human operators only
need to specify a set of goals

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:34.180
to be accomplished and
then have planners produce

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.910
a series of actions that
achieve the mission goals,

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.880
based on the model of the
physical plant under control,

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.700
the environment,
and the objectives.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.220
The planner integration
project combines

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.910
the capabilities of two planners
to provide a robust planning

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:49.900
framework.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.380
We demonstrate the integration's
capabilities on Odyssey IV,

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.330
an autonomous
underwater vehicle,

00:00:55.330 --> 00:01:00.850
or AUV, designed and built by
the MIT AUV Lab at Sea Grant.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:05.260
The AUV has the ability to
dive, ascend, and glide,

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.530
as well as to take
pictures and CTD readings.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:12.280
We can specify a set of tasks
for the AUV to complete.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:15.850
The interface allows us to place
start and finish locations,

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:19.600
manipulate obstacles,
place waypoints, and enable

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.560
discrete actions such as taking
a picture or recording video.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.290
Here, a user is creating a
simple task involving three way

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.410
points surrounding an obstacle.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:30.910
After setting the
starting point,

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:33.520
the user specifies the
desired waypoint surrounding

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.980
the obstacle and also
moves the goal point

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.000
to the desired location.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.640
The yellow edges
between the nodes

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.090
represent the high level plan
designed by the operator, which

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.490
crosses an obstacle in its way.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:47.710
Kirk calculates the
first layer of the plan

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:51.460
to go around the obstacle,
shown here as the green path.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.280
Finally, the red path
shows the complete plan,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.010
which is the result of
Kong Ming refinement.

00:01:57.010 --> 00:02:00.920
The AUV executed the plan
successfully in open water.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.060
The final trajectory shows the
initial movement of the AUV

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.640
to the start, followed
by the wavy artifacts

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.169
from the control.

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:10.449
As a more difficult
task, the AUV

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:14.140
was tasked with drawing
a K in the ocean.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:17.900
The AUV executed the plan
successfully in open water.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.100
The actual trajectory is
plotted in Google Earth.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.650
The Planner Integration
Project shows not only

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:26.530
the successful integration
of Kirk and Kong Ming,

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:29.800
but their interoperation with
a vehicle in a real world

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.950
environment.

